onstraint. The 600-unit budget is ~1.6% of the queried window, making an accidental alignment of two isolated events unlikely (~0.8%). The static connection is best explained by 886 being a 35-partner, 54-triangle broker rather than by genuine temporal propagation; activity volume is also ruled out since 1885 and 1460 are low-degree nodes far from the activity leaders."}, "raw_final_response": "## Analysis\n\n**Structure first.** Node 1885 has only 6 out-neighbours (step_03) and node 1460 only 3 in-neighbours (step_04). The intersection is exactly one node: **886**. There is no direct 1885\u21921460 edge (step_14), so any cascade must run 1885 \u2192 886 \u2192 1460.\n\n**The candidate path is maximally fragile.** Both required hops fired exactly once over the entire 100k horizon: 1885\u2192886 has 1 event (step_06), 886\u21921460 has 1 event (step_07). There is no repetition to give the chain a second chance at satisfying the \u2264600 gap, consistent with the graph's very low repeat ratio of 0.031 (step_01). Node 1460 sits in zero triangles (step_12), so there is no alternate/redundant 2-hop route to it; the other two in-neighbours of 1460 (765, 1045) have in-neighbourhoods (steps 09, 10) that are disjoint from 1885's out-neighbourhood (step_03), confirming 886 is the sole broker.\n\n**Confounder controls.** The apparent connectivity is largely a hub artefact: 886 is a 35-partner broker (steps 08, 11) with 54 incident triangles (step_12), and 1885's other out-neighbours include high-degree hubs 1613 (141 events) and 579 (74 events) (step_11). Static reachability through such hubs is expected regardless of timing. Neither 1885 (12 events) nor 1460 (6 events) is anywhere near the activity leaders (step_13), so \"volume\" cannot be doing the work either. The 600-unit budget covers only ~1.6% of the 38,400-unit query window; two single, uncoordinated events aligning in that order and gap is roughly a 0.8% coincidence (step_15).\n\n**Conclusion.** A single-shot, single-broker chain with no repeat firings and no redundant paths does not constitute evidence of a time-respecting cascade; the static connection is explained by hub brokerage.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"task_id\": \"t_9cf28e7b\",\n  \"decision\": \"absent\",\n  \"mechanism\": \"cascade\",\n  \"entities\": [1885, 886, 1460],\n  \"window\": [3683, 42083],\n  \"confidence\": 0.6,\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_03\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_04\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_14\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_06\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_07\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_01\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_15\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_09\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_10\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_08\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_11\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_12\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_16\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_13\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"}\n  ],\n  \"narrative\": \"The only route from 1885 to 1460 is the 2-hop path through the single shared node 886; there is no direct edge. Each required hop occurred exactly once in the whole horizon, and 1460 participates in no triangles, so there is no redundant or repeated path that could satisfy the 600-unit relay constraint. The 600-unit budget is ~1.6% of the queried window, making an accidental alignment of two isolated events unlikely (~0.8%). The static connection is best explained by 886 being a 35-partner, 54-triangle broker rather than by genuine temporal propagation; activity volume is also ruled out since 1885 and 1460 are low-degree nodes far from the activity leaders.\"\n}\n```", "total_input_tokens": 42026, "total